## Story Description
The get_by_month() endpoint in backend/app/routers/cube.py at line 263 accepts no query params beyond engagement_id — it has no Optional imports or Query() params. SupplierPage.tsx already passes supplier_id as a query param when fetching per-supplier monthly data for the detail Sheet (line 84-86), but the server ignores it and always returns engagement-wide totals. The fix is to add supplier_id as an optional Query param, then build the SQL WHERE clause dynamically using the same where_clauses list + params dict pattern used in get_by_supplier(). When supplier_id is None, behaviour is identical to today. When set, add AND canonical_supplier_id = :supplier_id.

## Acceptance Criteria
- Edit file: backend/app/routers/cube.py — confirm 'from typing import Optional' is already imported at the top of the file (it is used by other endpoints); if not, add it
- Edit file: backend/app/routers/cube.py — in get_by_month() function signature, add supplier_id: Optional[str] = Query(default=None) as a new parameter after engine: Engine = Depends(_engine). Keep the existing user_email and engine dependencies.
- Edit file: backend/app/routers/cube.py — in get_by_month(), replace the hardcoded SQL string passed to conn.execute() with a dynamic WHERE clause builder. Build where_clauses = ['engagement_id = :eid', 'COALESCE(is_intercompany, 0) = 0', 'COALESCE(is_tax_line, 0) = 0', 'invoice_date IS NOT NULL'] and params = {'eid': engagement_id}. If supplier_id is not None, append 'canonical_supplier_id = :supplier_id' and set params['supplier_id'] = supplier_id. Build where_sql = ' AND '.join(where_clauses). Use where_sql in the SQL text.
- Edit file: backend/app/routers/cube.py — pass params to conn.execute(text(...), params) instead of the hardcoded {'eid': engagement_id}
- Run: cd backend && python -m pytest tests/test_cube_api.py -v — all existing tests must pass

